\section{Covariate matrices}{

\code{linear_covs}, \code{emax_covs}, and \code{ec50_covs} accept numeric matrices,
factors, character vectors, or data frames (possibly mixing numeric and
categorical columns).  Categorical variables are automatically expanded via
\code{\link[=model.matrix]{model.matrix()}} using treatment contrasts: for a factor with \eqn{k}
levels the reference level is absorbed by the base parameter (\eqn{E_0},
\eqn{E_{max}}, or \eqn{\log EC_{50}}) and \eqn{k-1} contrast columns are
added.  All inputs must have \code{length(y)} rows.  Column names label the
coefficients; unnamed columns receive labels \code{"V1"}, \code{"V2"}, etc.
When predicting with categorical covariates, pass new data in the same form
(factor or data frame); \code{\link[=predict.logistic_emax]{predict.logistic_emax()}} re-applies the training
contrasts and reference levels automatically.
}

\section{Exposure levels}{

\code{x} must be finite and non-negative, and \code{y} must contain both outcome
classes. E0, Emax and EC50 need at least 3 distinct exposure values
(counting zero), so fewer is an error. With exactly 3 the curve is exactly
determined and EC50 is usually poorly estimated, so in addition to the
\strong{Sample size checks} a warning is given when \code{x} has fewer than 4
distinct values. For factor covariates, the sparse-level check uses the
original levels. The EPV count is all parameters except E0. EPV rules of
thumb were developed for models with a linear predictor. Emax and EC50
enter this model nonlinearly, so treat EPV >= 10 as a minimum.
}

\section{Convergence and standard errors}{

When \code{start} is \code{NULL}, the optimiser is run from three EC50 starting
values (the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of the non-zero exposures) and
the best optimum is kept. The result is then finished with Newton steps.
\code{converged} is \code{TRUE} only when \code{\link[=optim]{optim()}} reports success, the Hessian at
the optimum is positive definite and well conditioned, and the remaining
possible gain in log-likelihood (the Newton decrement) is below 1e-4.
Otherwise a warning explains which check failed. When the Hessian check
fails, some parameters are not identified (for example EC50 far above the
highest exposure, where Emax and EC50 trade off), and standard errors are
reported as \code{NA} instead of as misleadingly precise values. In weakly
identified designs, consider a profile likelihood or \code{\link[=bootstrap_emax]{bootstrap_emax()}}
for EC50 rather than the Wald standard error.
}

\section{Sample size checks}{

The model is checked against the number of events it is fitted to. Any
problems are reported together in one warning of class
\code{bootlace_sample_size_warning}. The diagnostics are also stored in the
returned object as \code{sample_size} and summarised by the print method.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Events per variable (EPV).} Events are the number of events for a Cox
model, or the number in the less frequent outcome class for a binary
model. Variables are the estimated coefficients other than the intercept,
so each factor contrast counts. A warning is given when EPV is below 10.
Below that, Peduzzi et al. (1996) found biased regression coefficients
and confidence intervals without proper coverage. \code{\link[=stepwise_reduce]{stepwise_reduce()}}
uses stricter limits for variable selection.
\item \strong{Sparse factor levels.} A level of a categorical predictor with no
events (or, for a binary outcome, no non-events). This usually means
separation or an infinite estimate.
\item \strong{Too few events overall.} Fewer than 20 events in the less frequent
outcome class. This floor is this package's own pragmatic choice, not a
published standard.
}

These are rough rules of thumb. The strength of the effects and the
correlation between predictors also affect how accurate the estimates are
(Heinze et al., 2018, Section 2.1.3). To silence the warning once you have
considered it, wrap the call in
\verb{withCallingHandlers(<call>, bootlace_sample_size_warning = function(w) invokeRestart("muffleWarning"))}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(42)
n    <- 200
dose <- sort(rep(c(0, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100), length.out = n))
eta  <- qlogis(0.10) + (qlogis(0.75) - qlogis(0.10)) * dose / (10 + dose)
y    <- rbinom(n, 1, plogis(eta))

# Basic model
fit <- fit_logistic_emax(y, dose)
print(fit)

# Linear covariate on the link scale
wt  <- rnorm(n, 70, 10)
fit2 <- fit_logistic_emax(y, dose, linear_covs = cbind(weight = wt))
print(fit2)

# Covariate modifying Emax (binary group as numeric)
grp <- rbinom(n, 1, 0.5)
fit3 <- fit_logistic_emax(y, dose, emax_covs = cbind(group = grp))
print(fit3)

# Categorical covariate: factor passed directly (no pre-expansion needed)
trt <- factor(sample(c("placebo", "low", "high"), n, replace = TRUE))
fit4 <- fit_logistic_emax(y, dose, linear_covs = data.frame(treatment = trt))
print(fit4)

}
